## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Classification

Your code is a **timeout enforcement** pattern with a **ThreadAbortException/ResetAbort** catch block. Three APIs must go:

| Removed API | Why |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `ThreadAbortException` catch | Never thrown — dead code |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`) |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when
            // the token fires, even if work() is still running.
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ae) when (ae.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ae.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Changes Explained

1. **`CancellationTokenSource(timeout)`** replaces the manual `Thread.Join` + `Thread.Abort` pattern — it auto-cancels after the timeout.
2. **`Task.Run`** replaces manual `Thread` creation — integrates with the thread pool.
3. **`Task.WaitAsync(token)`** (.NET 6+) gives cancellable waiting — the caller unblocks immediately when the timeout fires, even if `work()` is still running.
4. **`OperationCanceledException`** replaces the `ThreadAbortException` catch.

### ⚠️ Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Details |
|---|---|
| **Work doesn't stop automatically** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work()` is a CPU-bound loop, it keeps running in the background after timeout. To fix this, pass `CancellationToken` into `work` and check it at regular intervals. |
| **Signature change needed for true cancellation** | For the work to actually stop, change the signature to `Func<CancellationToken, T>` and forward the token. See the improved version below. |
| **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaking it causes timer leaks. Always `using`. |
| **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when` filter ensures you only catch timeout-triggered cancellation, not caller-initiated cancellation from an outer scope. |

### Improved Version (Truly Cooperative)

If you can modify the work delegates to accept a token:

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

        try
        {
            return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}

// Caller example — work cooperates by checking the token:
var executor = new TimedExecutor();
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in hugeList)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return "done";
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

This ensures work **actually stops** when the timeout fires, rather than continuing to burn CPU in the background — the fundamental difference between preemptive (`Thread.Abort`) and cooperative (`CancellationToken`) cancellation.
